//? Title: Fitting and querying a discrete Bayesian network
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Simulate a binary treatment and outcome with a shared cause. Fit conditional
probability tables using a Dirichlet prior and compare observational
conditioning to an intervention. Fit a Chow–Liu tree to the same data to
explore an alternative probabilistic factorization.
)

> Specify the network factorization

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/discrete_network_dag.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const datamunge::causal::DAG graph(3, {{0, 1}, {0, 2}, {1, 2}});
    graph.diagram({{5, 3}, {0, 0}, {10, 0}}, {"Shared cause", "Treatment", "Outcome"})
        .save_svg("build/discrete_network_dag.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/discrete_network_dag.svg)
// result_end

> Fit probabilities and compare conditioning with intervention

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/discrete_network.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace dm = datamunge;

int main() {
    const dm::causal::DAG graph(3, {{0, 1}, {0, 2}, {1, 2}});
    const dm::bayes::DiscreteBayesianNetwork truth(graph, {2, 2, 2},
        {{.5, .5}, {.8, .2, .2, .8}, {.9, .1, .7, .3, .5, .5, .3, .7}});
    const auto data = truth.sample(3000, 119);
    const auto fit = dm::bayes::fit_discrete_network(graph, {2, 2, 2}, data);
    const auto tree = dm::bayes::fit_chow_liu(data, {2, 2, 2});
    std::vector<double> conditional, interventional, generating;
    for (const std::size_t treatment : {0, 1}) {
        conditional.push_back(fit.marginal(2, {{1, treatment}})[1]);
        interventional.push_back(fit.marginal(2, {}, {{1, treatment}})[1]);
        generating.push_back(truth.marginal(2, {}, {{1, treatment}})[1]);
    }
    std::cout << "Fitted intervention contrast: " << interventional[1] - interventional[0]
              << ". Chow–Liu predictive outcome probability: " << tree.marginal(2)[1] << ".\n";
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
        .lines({0, 1}, conditional, "Observed treatment group", {207, 82, 57})
        .lines({0, 1}, interventional, "Fitted intervention", {36, 105, 172})
        .points({0, 1}, generating, "Generating intervention")
        .title("Outcome probability by treatment").x_label("Treatment state")
        .x_ticks({0, 1}, {"0", "1"})
        .y_label("Probability of outcome = 1").save_svg("build/discrete_network.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Fitted intervention contrast: 0.189647. Chow–Liu predictive outcome probability: 0.394143.
\image(figures/discrete_network.svg)
// result_end

Conditioning on treatment changes the distribution of the shared cause.
Fixing treatment by intervention preserves the shared cause's marginal
distribution and replaces the treatment mechanism. The generating contrast
under intervention is 0.2. The Chow–Liu tree chooses edges by empirical
mutual information and orients them from a chosen root for factorization.
The causal interpretation in this example comes from the supplied DAG.
